      "synthesized_program": "def get_seed_coordinate (grid ):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the (row, col) coordinate of the single non-zero pixel in the grid.\n    \"\"\"\n    for r ,row in enumerate (grid ):\n        for c ,val in enumerate (row ):\n            if val !=0 :\n                return (r ,c )\n    return None\n\ndef transform (grid :list [list [int ]])->list [list [int ]]:\n    rows =len (grid )\n    cols =len (grid [0 ])\n\n    # Find the seed coordinate\n    r_seed ,c_seed =-1 ,-1 \n    for r in range (rows ):\n        for c in range (cols ):\n            if grid [r ][c ]!=0 :\n                r_seed ,c_seed =r ,c \n                break \n        if r_seed !=-1 :\n            break \n\n    if r_seed ==-1 :\n        return [row [:]for row in grid ]\n\n        # Create a copy of the grid to modify\n    new_grid =[row [:]for row in grid ]\n    color =grid [r_seed ][c_seed ]\n\n    # Determine if we extend horizontally or vertically\n    # Based on the hypothesis: \"row if the seed is the only element in its column, \n    # or column if the seed is the only element in its row\"\n    # In a single-seed grid, both conditions are technically true.\n    # Looking at Example 1: Seed at (2,0) -> Row 2 is filled.\n    # Looking at Example 2: Seed at (5,4) -> Col 4 is filled.\n    # Looking at Example 3: Seed at (0,2) -> Col 2 is filled.\n\n    # Logic check: If seed is at (r, c), and we want to fill a line:\n    # Example 1: (2,0) -> fills row 2.\n    # Example 2: (5,4) -> fills col 4.\n    # Example 3: (0,2) -> fills col 2.\n\n    # The rule \"row if seed is only element in its column\" implies:\n    # If column has only one non-zero, fill row.\n    # If row has only one non-zero, fill column.\n    # Since there is only ONE non-zero pixel total, both are true.\n    # However, the examples show:\n    # Ex 1: (2,0) -> Row 2 filled. (Seed is at edge of row/col)\n    # Ex 2: (5,4) -> Col 4 filled.\n    # Ex 3: (0,2) -> Col 2 filled.\n\n    # Let's refine: If the seed is the only element in its column, fill the row.\n    # In a grid with only one non-zero, the seed is ALWAYS the only element in its column.\n    # But Example 2 and 3 fill columns. \n    # Let's look at the seed position:\n    # Ex 1: (2,0) -> Row 2. (c=0)\n    # Ex 2: (5,4) -> Col 4. (r=5)\n    # Ex 3: (0,2) -> Col 2. (r=0)\n    # It seems if r is at the boundary (0 or max), it fills the column. \n    # If c is at the boundary (0 or max), it fills the row.\n    # Wait, Ex 1: c=0, fills row. Ex 2: r=5, fills col. Ex 3: r=0, fills col.\n    # If c == 0, fill row. If r == 0 or r == max, fill col.\n\n    # Simpler pattern: If c == 0, fill row. Else, fill column.\n    if c_seed ==0 :\n        for c in range (cols ):\n            new_grid [r_seed ][c ]=color \n    else :\n        for r in range (rows ):\n            new_grid [r ][c_seed ]=color \n\n    return new_grid",
